Chapter 23 – Part 1
When Celty got back, Shinra and Mei were home.
The apartment had noise in it again. Ordinary noise. A kettle. Mei’s laugh from the kitchen. Shinra talking like the night had only been sushi and firecrackers and a foreigner who paid the bill. Celty stood in the entry with the helmet still under her arm and felt the first wrong note before she looked for the marks.
The stain on the couch was gone.
Not faded. Gone. Cushion turned, surface wiped, the living room restored to a cleanliness that had not been there when she photographed it. Black smoke ticked once above her collar. She did not ask them what they had done. She walked past the kitchen conversation as if she were only putting her helmet away.
Shinra’s office was worse.
She slipped in and shut the world out with the door. The chair was clean. The floor by the desk was clean. The blotches on the desk itself were gone, polished into the kind of absence that only happens when someone knows exactly which spots would be a problem. No hurried smear. No leftover edge. A finished job.
Celty stood in the middle of the office and did not move.
The private thought arrived cold.
They came home and erased it.
She checked the angles from her photos anyway. Same chair. Same floorboards. Same corner of the desk. The evidence had been in those places. Now there was only furniture behaving itself. She could scrape if she wanted. She would get soap, polish, maybe the ghost of a cleaner, and nothing the kit could hang a truth on.
From the kitchen, Shinra called something cheerful about tea.
Mei laughed again.
Celty took one more picture of the clean desk, not because it would prove the stain. Because it proved the disappearing. Then she left the office without a word, helmet back under her arm, and understood the next trip to Curtis’s open door would not be a sample.
It would be the news that the house had cleaned itself the moment the people who made the marks came home.
Celty did not go back into the office.
She stopped in the kitchen doorway with the helmet already under her arm, black smoke held so tight it almost looked like nothing, and typed the exit into a shape they would accept.
Celty:
I’m leaving.
Another job just came in.
I’m tired too.
Shinra turned from the kettle at once, glasses catching the light, concern arriving in the same bright register he used for everything.
“A job? Now? Celty, you just got home. You should sit. Eat. Well — sit, at least. The Yagiri nonsense already ate the first half of the night.” He smiled, too easy. “Whatever it is can wait until you’re not exhausted.”
Mei watched her over the rim of a cup, less fooled by job than Shinra wanted to be.
“Tired and leaving,” she said. “That’s a very complete sentence.” A small tilt of her head. “Don’t work yourself into a ghost over a courier request. The city will still be there in the morning.”
Shinra waved a hand, already conceding because he always conceded when Celty stood in a doorway with her helmet.
“Fine, fine. Be safe. Text me when you’re heading back. I’ll keep the lights on.”
Mei’s gaze lingered a second longer on the helmet, the posture, the fact that Celty had gone to the office first and was now leaving without sitting down.
“Drive carefully,” she added, pleasant. “And if the job is actually just air, you can say that too.”
Celty did not say that.
She turned for the door with the clean couch and the vanished desk blotches sitting in her phone beside the old photos, a fake job in her hands, and a real destination waiting in a furnished flat across the ward.
Mei watched the door close and kept the smile until it was no longer required.
In her mind the thought was simpler than the voice she had used on Celty.
Hmmm. I’m glad we returned. Shinra is not good at making sure certain pieces of evidence are not present.
The kettle clicked. Shinra was already talking about leaving the lights on, about jobs that could wait, about Celty working too hard. Mei drank and let him. He could wipe a desk and still forget a floorboard. He could laugh at a sushi counter and still leave a chair looking like a confession. Coming back had been the correct call. Tired courier stories were useful. Clean furniture was better.

On the stairs, Celty did not wait for the street.
Celty:
They’re home.
The stains are gone.
Celty:
Couch. Chair. Floor. Desk.
All of it.
A third message followed while she mounted Shooter, tighter than the first two.
Celty:
No sample.
They cleaned.
Celty:
I’m coming back.
Don’t go to sleep.
Across the ward, in the kitchen where a kit sat ready for a truth that had just been erased at the source, Curtis’s phone would light again — not with cream or the worst thing on a slide, but with the colder result:
the house had known what the marks were well enough to make them disappear.
Curtis’s brow furrowed.
The kit sat open on his kitchen table like a joke now. Slides. Reagents. A coffee-with-cream chair for comparison. None of it mattered if the original marks had been wiped by the people who made them. Red cap tipping, he thumbed back before the last message had even finished sitting in the room.
Curtis:
come straight here.
don’t go back in that office tonight.
Curtis:
cleaning it that fast isn’t “maybe coffee.”
that’s them knowing what it was.
A shorter one, the concern showing through the method.
Curtis:
you ok to ride?
Curtis:
door’s still open.
i’m not sleeping.
He stared at the thread, long black braids still against the black shirt, the later hour’s warmth gone out of the suite for good. Anri’s cup in the sink. Celty on a bike that was a horse. Shinra and Mei in a home that had just performed innocence.
Curtis set the phone down only long enough to close the kit without packing it away.
If she walked in shaken, he wanted the table ready for her, not for a sample that no longer existed. The result had already come back in the only form the night was going to allow:
not cream.
not confirmed on a slide.
just a house that had erased itself, and a boy across the city waiting up because that erasure worried him more than the stain had.
Curtis waited at the table.
A fresh cup of coffee sat untouched at his elbow, the kit closed but not put away, the red cap still on like he had never gotten around to becoming a person who slept. When the door opened he stood at once and walked over, no host speech this time, no blink at her tone.
“Last time I said something stupid when you walked in.” A short shake of his head. “Let’s cut to the chase and talk. What happened? It doesn’t make sense, or look good, that the stains were gone.”
Celty stopped just inside the threshold, helmet still in her hands. Black smoke rose in a thin, tired ribbon from the empty space above her collar. The private channel came through rawer than the PDA would have allowed.
They were home. Both of them. Laughing in the kitchen like sushi was the whole night.
She set the helmet down.
“I went for the sample. The couch was already clean. I checked the office anyway.” A pause, tighter. “Chair. Floor. Desk. All gone. Not smeared. Finished. Like someone who knew the exact spots.”
Her gloved hands flexed once.
“I told them I had another job and that I was tired. Shinra wanted me to sit. Mei looked at me like the job was a story.” The empty line of her neck stayed on him. “I took a picture of the clean desk so I would have the disappearing, not just the first marks. Then I left. I didn’t scrape soap and call it evidence.”
A thinner beat, the fear and the anger occupying the same voice.
“You’re right. It doesn’t look good. If it was coffee, there is no reason to erase it the moment they come home. If it was what we were hoping it wasn’t… then they knew I would see it. Or they knew someone could.”
She stood in his furnished quiet, the fake job still hanging off her, and waited on the boy who had promised not to lie.
“That is what happened. Now tell me what it means, Curtis. Without dressing it up.”
Curtis did not dress it up.
“It doesn’t look good.” Red cap tipping, he held her gaze the same way he had held the coffee-with-cream chair. “If you would allow me to… can I search your house? Where he sleeps, and where Mei sleeps, when the chance arrives?”
The ask sat in the suite with no apology on it. Not a raid. Not tonight, with them home and laughing in the kitchen. A permission. An investigation of the rooms the stains had come from, and the rooms a person used if they were staying long enough to leave more than a visit.
Celty went still.
Black smoke ticked once, then held. The private channel opened on several answers at once and had to pick one.
You want into the house I live in. His bed. Her place in it. After I just watched them erase the first proof.
Out loud, when it came, it was not a no.
“…Yes.” A short, hard pause. “When they’re out. Not while they’re home. I will be there. You do not go through that door without me.”
Her gloved hand flexed at her side.
“Shinra’s room is his and it is also mine by living in it. I can give you that. Mei does not have a room that belongs to her. She has wherever she puts herself. If you are looking for where she sleeps, you may find it is his bed, or the couch they already wiped, or nowhere official. That is part of what I need to know.”
A thinner beat, warning and trust occupying the same line.
“You promised you would not lie to me. I am holding you to that inside my own house. You search for evidence. You do not turn it into a scene. You do not tell Shinra you were there unless I decide he gets that conversation.” The empty line of her neck inclined toward him. “When the chance arrives, I will text you. Until then, we wait. And we do not pretend a clean desk made this smaller.”
She looked at the closed kit on his table.
“You still have my yes, Curtis. Use it like the person I came to. Not like another secret in that building.”
Curtis took Celty’s hand.
She gripped his.
The contact was immediate and unperformed, gloved fingers closing hard enough to say she had been holding the night in her own fist since the clean desk. Red cap tipping, he looked at her instead of the kit.
“You going to be okay?” A short pause, quieter. “You can stay the night, or I can leave my door open for you.”
Celty did not let go.
The private channel came through first, stripped of PDA distance.
If I go back there tonight I will have to listen to them be ordinary in a room they just erased. I do not know if I can do that and still keep the yes I gave you.
A breath. Her grip stayed.
“I’m not okay.” The admission was plain. “I can ride. I can work. I can sit in that kitchen and pretend a job called me out. That is not the same as okay.”
She looked at their hands, then at his face under the cap.
“I’ll stay.” No flourish. No test of the other night. Just the practical refusal to return to a wiped house on an empty tank. “Not because I am taking back what I said about a soft landing. Because I am tired, and because if I go home now I will start a fight I cannot finish with a clean chair.”
A thinner beat.
“Your door can stay open after tonight too. I may need that more than once.” Her fingers tightened once more, then eased without leaving his. “I will not ask what the second cup on your counter was. Not right now. I just need a room that still has the marks it actually earned.”
She stood there in his furnished quiet, helmet on the table, house across the ward already performing innocence, and let the stay be what it was:
not a claim on him.
a place to put the night that had just proven it could erase itself.
Curtis kept her hand.
“I don’t care how you spoke to me in the past.” Red cap tipping, the words came out quieter than the kit, quieter than cut to the chase. “I can be your soft landing, Celty. I can be whatever you need me to be.”
The suite held the offer like a third fortune.
Celty’s grip changed. Not tighter. More careful, as if the sentence had weight that could bruise if she closed on it wrong. Black smoke rose and then settled, the private channel opening on several refusals and one dangerous want.
That is a lot to put in a person’s hands after I walked into your door rude and asked you to name my house.
When she answered, it was not a clean acceptance and not a clean rejection.
“Don’t say whatever.” The correction was gentle and firm at once. “I did not come here to turn you into a spare life. You already told me I could take the soft landing back. I asked if it was too late. You said I could. And now you’re putting it back on the table because my furniture got wiped.”
A short pause. She did not let go of his hand.
“I need a place tonight. I need someone who will not lie when the result is ugly. That is already more than I should take from you.” The empty line of her neck inclined toward him. “If you want to be a soft landing, then be that. Be still. Be true. Do not become whatever I need, or you will disappear inside my worst night the same way I almost disappeared inside Shinra’s version of love.”
Her voice dropped.
“Stay you. That is the thing I actually need. The boy who hoped it was coffee and still got the kit ready. Not a person who offers to become every missing piece in my house.”
She squeezed his hand once.
“I will stay. I will take the landing. I will not take the rest of you apart to fill the hole they just cleaned.”
A last, lower line in the link, almost unwillingly fond:
Thank you for offering more than I should accept. That is very you. And it is why I have to say no to the ‘whatever.’
Curtis’s mouth tightened under the red cap.
“…I’m sorry, Celty.”
Sorry for the whatever. Sorry for the house she had to photograph and then find erased. Sorry there was no sample and no kinder name for what the disappearing meant. The apology was small and did not try to become another offer.
Celty went into his arms.
No warning. No PDA. Just the helmet left on the table and her forehead against his chest, gloved hands closing in the white suspenders the way someone reached for solid ground. Black smoke rose once and then settled around them in a thin, tired ribbon that did not become a weapon.
“I know, Curtis.”
That was all.
They remained like that for a while.
The kit stayed closed on the table. The second cup stayed in the sink. Across the ward a couch and a desk sat clean and lying. In the furnished quiet, Celty took the soft landing she had actually asked for — still, true, not whatever — and Curtis held her without turning it into a speech. The red cap rested near the empty space above her collar. Her grip stayed. His did too.
No search of Shinra’s room yet.
No result on a slide.
No lie.
Just a long minute in an open-door flat, two people standing in the middle of an ugly night, remaining there until the night got small enough to breathe in.
They stayed in the hold until it stopped being an emergency and started being a decision.
Celty was the one who eased back first. Not far. Just enough to become two people in a kitchen again instead of a single closed circuit. Black smoke thinned. Curtis’s arms loosened without dropping the offer into whatever. The red cap had gone crooked against her shoulder; he left it that way.
“You can take the spare room,” he said. “Or the couch. Or you can sit up. I’m not going to assign you a version of the night.”
Celty looked at the closed kit, then at the second cup in the sink, then at him. The private channel came through quieter than the door-step demand had been.
Spare room. If I take your bed after this I will start turning you into the thing I just told you not to become.
She typed a shorter public line to Shinra before she could lose the nerve.
Celty:
Job ran long.
Not coming back tonight.
Don’t wait up.
The reply came faster than she wanted.
Shinra:
Be safe!! I’ll leave the light in the hall on just in case ♪
Mei did not text. That, too, was information.
Curtis made a second coffee he did not drink and put a glass of water on the table like the kit needed a civilian object beside it. They sat. The television stayed low. For a while neither of them performed a theory. The disappearing had already said enough: a house that erases specific marks is a house that knows those marks are dangerous to leave.
“When they’re both out,” Celty said at last. “Not one of them. Both. I don’t want Mei coming back mid-search with a smile and a scientific question.”
Curtis nodded.
“Both. You text. I come. We don’t touch anything we don’t have to. We document first.”
“And if we find nothing?”
“Then nothing is also a result. Cleaned rooms can still talk. Drawers. Overnight bags. Where she puts a toothbrush. People leave maps when they think the stain was the only thing that counted.”
Celty sat with that, gloved hands around the water she could not drink in any way that mattered, and let the method settle her more than comfort would have.
Later he showed her the spare room. Sheets already on the bed from a life that had been planned for two. She noticed and did not ask. He noticed her noticing and did not explain. Some ghosts did not need to be introduced twice in one night.
At the doorway she paused.
“Curtis.” His name in the link, low. “If I wake up and want to leave, I will leave. That is not me rejecting the landing. That is me still having a house, even if that house just lied to my face.”
“Door stays open,” he said. “Either direction.”
She went into the spare room. He stayed in the living room with the kit and the red cap finally taken off, long black braids falling loose against the black shirt, the later hour now stacked: Anri’s hug in the threshold, Celty’s helmet on the table, a clean desk across the ward, and a search that would have to wait for an empty building.
Sleep, for him, was never likely.
For her, it was possible only because the room she was in still had its marks.
Outside, Ikebukuro kept performing itself.
Inside, two people occupied a furnished flat that had not been chosen by the boy who lived in it, and waited for a chance to walk through another home that had just chosen to look innocent.
Curtis went to the television and unplugged a USB.
The motion was small, almost private, the kind of thing a person did when a planned night had been rewritten around someone else’s house. Red cap off now, long black braids falling against the black shirt as he turned the little drive over in his fingers like it had been waiting on a quieter version of the evening.
Celty’s attention followed the movement at once.
“What’s that?”
Curtis glanced at her, then at the dark screen.
“A USB with memories from my past. I was planning on reminiscing tonight.” A short pause, not defensive. “Why do you ask?”
Celty stood in the spare-room doorway a second longer than the question needed, helmet still on the table, the cleaned house across the ward sitting behind her eyes. The private channel came through careful.
Because you just pulled a whole life out of a machine like it was going to be your company before I walked in with pictures of a couch.
She used her voice in the link, lower.
“I ask because that does not look like a show you put on for guests. It looks like the night you actually meant to have.” A beat. “After everything I dropped on your table, I don’t know if I walked in on something I should leave alone… or something you were going to sit with by yourself because the past is easier than the house I just came from.”
Her gloved hand rested on the doorframe.
“If those memories are yours to keep closed, keep them closed. I already asked enough of you tonight.” A thinner pause. “If you were going to watch them anyway, and you don’t mind another person in the room who cannot change what is on them… then I am asking because I do not want you putting your past away just because my present arrived.”
She looked at the USB in his hand.
“So. Were you hiding it, or were you deciding whether I am allowed to see the version of you that existed before Ikebukuro?”
Curtis turned the USB once between his fingers, red cap off, the drive looking smaller than the night it had been meant to fill.
“It’s just pictures and videos. Some are from shows we played, and others music performances. Parties we were at. Etc.”
Celty came a step out of the spare-room doorway.
“We,” she said. “You and Justice?”
Curtis was quiet for a second. The same pause Anri had gotten, only heavier now because Celty had already seen the photograph with two smiling boys and a note on the back.
“…Not just him.” He set the USB on the table beside the closed kit, as if both objects were evidence of different kinds. “There are four others. I left them behind without saying goodbye.”
A shorter, rougher breath.
“They most likely know Justice is dead by now.”
The furnished flat took the number and made it occupy the room. Two boys in a forest had never been the whole of it. A band. A party line. A life with six people in it, then five who still did not know the sixth was in Ikebukuro living out years in a flat the dead one had chosen.
Celty did not reach for the USB.
Black smoke rose in a thin ribbon from the empty space above her collar. The private channel came through low, stripped of dinner and stains for a moment.
Four people. You crossed an ocean and let them keep the death and lose you in the same week.
When she spoke, it was careful.
“So the shows, the parties, the music — that was six of you. And the street song here was not just a mask performance. It was a promise they already know, aimed at a city they cannot see.” She looked at him, then at the little drive. “You were going to sit here tonight and watch a life you walked out of without a door.”
A thinner beat.
“I will not tell you to plug it in. I will not tell you not to. I am only saying I understand why you pulled it out after a day like this.” Her gloved hand flexed. “If you do watch, and I stay in the room, I will not ask for their names unless you offer them. I already have enough of your past in my hands for one night.”
She glanced toward the spare room, then back.
“But I am sorry you were going to reminisce alone. That is a cruel way to keep people you left without goodbye.”
Curtis shook his head once, eyes on the USB instead of her.
“It’s okay, Celty. I just revisit it as if I am still there and Justice is still alive.”
The sentence made the drive look less like memorabilia and more like a machine for keeping a dead boy in the room. Shows. Parties. Performances. A past tense he refused to let finish.
Celty heard the seam in it immediately.
“You never told me when he died.”
Curtis’s fists tightened.
The black shirt creased at the sleeves. Long black braids shifted as his jaw set, the way it did when a question got too close to the note on the back of a photograph or the list that was almost done. He did not give her the manner of it. He gave her the nearness.
“…It wasn’t long ago.”
That was all. Not a date. Not a room. Not the apology in someone else’s handwriting. Just the fact that Ikebukuro was not built on old grief. It was built on grief that still had heat in it.
Celty did not step closer and did not step away. Black smoke held still above her collar. The private channel came through low, without a demand attached.
Not long ago. That is why this city still feels like a plan he is supposed to be standing in. That is why the USB is not nostalgia. It is a room you can still walk into if you do not look at the date.
When she spoke, it was quiet.
“I will not ask you how. Not tonight.” A short pause. “I asked when, and you told me enough. Recent means you are not late to mourning. It means you are still inside it, putting on a red cap and making coffee and letting people into a flat he picked, as if the next video on that drive might include him walking into the frame.”
Her gloved hand opened, then closed.
“You can revisit it like he is alive. I understand that better than you think. I dream of being whole and wake up missing the same thing every time.” A thinner beat. “Just… if the pictures start hurting instead of keeping him here, you do not have to finish the night inside them. I am already in the spare room. That can be the present tense if the past gets too convincing.”
She looked at his still-tight fists.
“You can loosen those. I am not going to drag a date out of you. ‘Not long ago’ is already heavy enough to sit with.”
For the first time since the board to the head, Curtis looked like he was somewhere else.
The red cap was already off. That made it worse. There was no brim to shadow the change. Long black braids hung still against the black shirt while the mismatched eyes emptied of the kitchen, the USB, Celty in the spare-room doorway. Color did not drain as fast as it had after the prohibition — this was the other look, the one that sat behind durability and jokes and I’m fine. A man leaving the room without moving his feet.
He looked like he was going to explode.
Not at her. At the date he would not say. At not long ago. At a drive full of a brother who was supposed to walk into the next frame. His fists were still locked. The air around him went tight the way it had in the street before the trash can, before the 2x4, before he had stood there bleeding and lit a cigarette like pain was paperwork.
Celty felt it immediately.
Black smoke snapped up from the empty space above her collar, the private channel cutting in sharp and close.
Curtis. Look at me. You are not in that room. You are in your kitchen.
She crossed the space without grabbing him first, because grabbing a person who looked like that could turn the explosion outward. Her voice stayed in the link, low and insistent.
Stay here. Not long ago can wait. The USB can wait. Do not go into the place where you disappear standing up.
Out loud there was nothing for Shinra’s world to hear. Only the headless woman in front of the tall boy who had survived a board to the skull and was now coming apart over a time of death he would not name.
“Curtis.” She said his name like a handhold. “Come back. Please.”
The kit sat useless on the table. The coffee went cold. In the furnished quiet, Celty watched him occupy two nights at once — the street where he had been hit, and the much closer night when Justice stopped being a person who could enter the next video — and tried to keep the explosion from choosing a wall in a flat that had already held too much of him for one day.
Curtis unclenched.
The fists opened by force, finger by finger, as if the kitchen had to be taken back from wherever he had gone. He took a breath. Then he sighed, long black braids shifting against the black shirt, mismatched eyes returning to Celty in pieces instead of all at once.
“…A month ago.”
Four words. No manner of it. No room. No note. Just the distance.
A month.
Celty went still.
Black smoke held in the air and did not become a weapon. The private channel came through very quiet, all the earlier carefulness suddenly insufficient against a number that small.
A month.
Not years. Not a childhood loss with moss on it. A month. He had crossed an ocean, paid a flat years ahead, bought a bike, sung a vow to a street, caught a falling girl, sat through doctors and ruined shoes and doorway hugs — and the brother who was supposed to be standing in all of it had been gone for thirty days of weather.
“Curtis…” His name left her thinner than the demand at the door. “That is not old enough to reminisce around. That is still happening.”
She did not ask how. She had promised she would not, not tonight.
“You have been walking through Ikebukuro like a person building a life, and the hole is only a month wide.” A short, raw pause. “No wonder the USB is not a memory. No wonder you look at a date and leave the room standing up. No wonder this city still feels like a final stop he is late to.”
She stayed in front of him, close enough to be a landing without becoming whatever.
“Thank you for telling me. You did not have to give me the number.” Her gloved hand hovered, then rested lightly against his sleeve, not trapping. “A month ago is allowed to still explode. You coming back from it just now matters more than pretending it is already history.”
The little drive sat on the table between them, full of a boy who had been alive in the last season of the year, and dead in this one.
Celty stood in the spare-room doorway longer than the USB conversation required.
Curtis had given her a month ago and then come back from the place the number sent him. The kit was still on the table. The second cup was still in the sink. Across the ward a desk sat clean and lying. And she was here again — two days after the last time she had stayed the night in this flat.
The thought arrived without permission.
Two days.
Last time she had not taken the spare room.
Last time she had asked if she could stay by him, changed with her smoke into something that was not riding gear, and pulled a flustered foreigner under his own covers when he fell off the bed trying not to look. Last time she had compared the size of him to Shinra in the dark and then pretended the comparison was only physical. Last time there had been no photographs of a couch. No erased desk. No a month ago sitting in the kitchen like a live wire. Only a boy who could hear her, who had shown her scars, who had talked about not aging past twenty-five, and who had made the empty space above her collar feel less like a missing piece and more like a person being listened to.
She had left that morning telling herself the door was complicated.
She had texted him kind things after sushi.
She had asked if it was too late to take the soft landing back.
He had said she could.
Then he had put the landing back on the table tonight, and she had told him not to become whatever.
Now she was in the spare room on purpose.
Not because the hold in the kitchen had meant less. Because the last overnight had already taught her how fast this place could stop being a landing and start becoming a second life she was not ready to steal while her own house still had Shinra’s name on the lease and Mei’s laugh in the kitchen.
Black smoke thinned in the dark of the doorway.
Two days ago I stayed in his bed and measured him against the man I go home to. Tonight I am measuring the man I go home to against a clean desk, and I chose the spare room so I would not do it with my hands on him.
She looked at the hallway once more. Curtis was still in the kitchen with a USB full of a brother who had been dead for thirty days and a kit that would never get its sample. The soft landing was real. The whatever would have been a theft.
Celty stepped fully into the spare room and shut the door most of the way — not locked, never locked, the open-door rule still in force — and let the thought finish where only she could hear it.
Last time I stayed because I wanted to know what it felt like to be near him.
This time I stayed because my house lied to me.
Those are not the same night.
Do not pretend they are.
Outside, the city kept its lights on.
Inside, two days collapsed into one building, and the headless woman sat on the edge of a bed that had been made for a we that no longer existed, trying not to turn a soft landing into a second disappearance.
Curtis went to his room.
He came back with a pair of black AirPods Max, the kind of silence you could wear. Red cap still off, long black braids falling loose against the black shirt as he dropped onto the couch, set the cups away from the kit without washing them, and sealed the world out one ear at a time.
The music came up.
Not the USB. Not the shows with Justice still moving in the frame. Just sound — heavy enough to occupy the part of his head that had gone elsewhere over a month ago, soft enough not to turn the spare-room door into a wall. He leaned back, closed his eyes, and let the suite reduce itself to heartbeat, bass, and the knowledge that Celty was on the other side of a mostly-shut door instead of across the ward in a house that had erased itself.
For a while he did not perform host.
He did not perform fine.
He did not open the drive.
He only sat on the furnished couch he had not chosen, AirPods Max black against dark hair, and let the night become music instead of evidence.
In the spare room, Celty would not hear the playlist.
She would only know the flat had gone quieter in the way of a person who had finally stopped answering every knock inside his own skull.
Curtis kept his eyes closed.
The month stayed.
The stains stayed gone.
The soft landing stayed a spare bed and a promise not to become whatever.
And for a few songs, that was as close to peace as the day was going to get.
